---
import ManualLayout from '../../layouts/ManualLayout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/paths';
---

<ManualLayout slug="git">
  <h2 id="why">Why Git</h2>
  <p>
    Because notes are plain text files, Git gives you a complete, inspectable history of your
    thinking for free — every version of every note, with the ability to go back. HelloNotes speaks
    Git directly, so you never need the command line, but everything it creates is a perfectly
    ordinary repository you can also use elsewhere.
  </p>

  <h2 id="init">Initialising a repository</h2>
  <p>
    Git is one of the collection’s rows at the top of the sidebar. The <strong>Git</strong> row is
    for the collection you have selected: it shows the current branch, with a dot beside it when
    there are uncommitted changes, and clicking it opens the Git pane. For a collection that is not
    yet a repository the row says so, and the pane offers <strong>Initialize Repository</strong>.
    After that the pane shows the current branch and how many files have changed. A collection
    opened directly from a cloud account is the provider’s to keep, so with one selected there is
    no Git row.
  </p>
  <p>
    To start a new collection as a repository, choose{' '}
    <strong>New Collection ▸ Git Repository…</strong> under the <strong>+</strong> at the top of
    the sidebar.
  </p>

  <h2 id="identity">Setting your identity</h2>
  <p>
    Commits need a name and email. Open the gear icon in the Git pane — or the{' '}
    <strong>Git</strong> tab of Settings — to set them for HelloNotes. If you set none, HelloNotes
    uses the repository’s own identity or your global one where it can read it, and otherwise your
    account’s name — which then travels with anything you push.
  </p>

  <h2 id="commit">Committing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Commit</strong> stages everything that changed and records it, under a message
      HelloNotes writes — “Update notes” and the date and time.
    </li>
    <li>
      A change you approve from the <a href={href('/manual/ai')}>Assistant</a> is committed too,
      whatever Auto-commit is set to — as a commit of its own, holding that note and nothing
      else. It is not made in a cloud folder, or when the collection is one folder of a larger
      repository, where Auto-commit is not allowed either — nor while another Git tool has
      something staged, which the commit would otherwise take with it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Auto-commit</strong> (an option in the pane) commits quietly in the background a
      little after you stop editing. It is off by default, and it <strong>never pushes</strong> —
      publishing is always something you do deliberately.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="history">Browsing and restoring history</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>History</strong>, in the right panel, lists the commits that touched the open note.
    Select one to read that version, and restore it if you want it back. Restoring writes the old
    content as a new change, so nothing in the history is lost — but it replaces the note as it
    stands, and Undo does not bring that back. HelloNotes asks before it does, so commit first if
    you have edits since the last commit that you want to keep. History looks back over the last
    300 commits.
  </p>

  <h2 id="remote">Syncing with a remote</h2>
  <p>
    To back up or sync between machines, connect a remote — GitHub, GitLab, or any Git host.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open Git settings (the gear in the Git pane) and add an account with a personal access token.</li>
    <li>
      Use <strong>Connect Remote</strong> in the pane, or clone an existing repository into a new
      collection with <strong>Open Collection ▸ from Repository…</strong> under the sidebar’s{' '}
      <strong>+</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Once there is a remote, the pane’s <strong>Sync</strong> menu has <strong>Push</strong>,
      which uploads your commits, and <strong>Fetch</strong>, which retrieves what is on the
      remote.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Use an <strong>HTTPS</strong> remote with a personal access token. Tokens are stored in the
      Keychain.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Cloning shows a progress indicator and can be stopped with <strong>Stop</strong> if you change
    your mind or the repository turns out to be larger than expected.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">Current limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No merge or pull.</strong> HelloNotes can fetch and push, but does not merge diverged
      histories. If two machines have both committed, reconcile them with a Git client or the
      command line.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Auto-commit is disabled in cloud folders</strong> — see{' '}
      <a href={href('/manual/cloud')}>Cloud storage</a> for why — and in a collection that is a
      folder inside a larger repository.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="tips">Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A private repository is a good backup: complete history, under your control, off your Mac.</li>
    <li>Add a <code>.gitignore</code> for <code>.DS_Store</code> and any large binaries you would rather not version.</li>
    <li>Push before switching machines; fetch when you arrive.</li>
  </ul>
</ManualLayout>
